# Content design

## The video follows a logical, well-organised flow that directly supports its stated objectives. 

A systematic review of 113 studies found empirical support for coherence and segmentation in instructional video and concluded that shorter, segmented and coherent videos paired with learning activities are more likely to improve learning.[^video-review]

Organise the video around explicit intended learning outcomes. Each section, explanation, example and visual should contribute to what learners are expected to know or perform. Remove content that is interesting but unnecessary. This applies the coherence principle: learners generally perform better when extraneous information is excluded. Use signalling cues to make the organisation of the essential content visible and guide attention towards relevant ideas and relationships.[^coherence]

Define the intended outcomes before scripting the video. Then select and sequence the explanations, demonstrations, examples and associated learning activities needed to elicit the expected learner performance. This reflects constructive alignment, in which outcomes, learning activities and assessment address the same intended capabilities.[^alignment]

Use a structure appropriate to the subject rather than enforcing a rigid template. A useful default may follow [BOPPPS][boppps-model] or [CARD][card-model] model and consist of a brief orientation, clearly identified sections, explicit transitions and a final synthesis or application prompt. Where they add value, state the purpose at the beginning and revisit the intended outcomes at the end. Use descriptive headings, chapter titles, spoken signposts, highlighting, arrows or brief animation to emphasise important information—not as decoration.[^signalling]

Break complex explanations into meaningful segments and allow learners to process one segment before moving to the next. A meta-analysis found small-to-moderate positive effects of learner-paced segmentation on retention and transfer.[^segmenting] Guidance for educational-video production similarly recommends keeping videos focused on their learning goals and using signals to highlight important concepts.[^video-guidelines]

[^coherence]: [Coherence and signalling principles](https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108894333.019 "Fiorella, L., & Mayer, R. E. (2021). Principles for Reducing Extraneous Processing in Multimedia Learning: Coherence, Signaling, Redundancy, Spatial Contiguity, and Temporal Contiguity Principles. In R. E. Mayer & L. Fiorella (Eds.), The Cambridge Handbook of Multimedia Learning, 3rd ed., pp. 185–198. Cambridge University Press.")

[^alignment]: [Constructive alignment](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00138871 "Biggs, J. (1996). Enhancing teaching through constructive alignment. Higher Education, 32, 347–364.")

[^signalling]: [The signalling or cueing principle](https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108894333.022 "van Gog, T. (2021). The Signaling (or Cueing) Principle in Multimedia Learning. In R. E. Mayer & L. Fiorella (Eds.), The Cambridge Handbook of Multimedia Learning, 3rd ed., pp. 221–230. Cambridge University Press.")

[^segmenting]: [Meta-analysis of the segmenting effect](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10648-018-9456-4 "Rey, G. D., Beege, M., Nebel, S., Wirzberger, M., Schmitt, T. H., & Schneider, S. (2019). A Meta-analysis of the Segmenting Effect. Educational Psychology Review, 31, 389–419.")

[^video-guidelines]: [Effective educational videos](https://doi.org/10.1187/cbe.16-03-0125 "Brame, C. J. (2016). Effective Educational Videos: Principles and Guidelines for Maximizing Student Learning from Video Content. CBE—Life Sciences Education, 15(4), es6.")

[^video-review]: [Systematic review of instructional-video design](https://doi.org/10.14742/ajet.7296 "Fyfield, M., Henderson, M., & Phillips, M. (2022). Improving Instructional Video Design: A Systematic Review. Australasian Journal of Educational Technology, 38(3), 155–183.")

[1]: https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108894333.019 "Principles for Reducing Extraneous Processing in Multimedia Learning (Chapter 14) - The Cambridge Handbook of Multimedia Learning"


